          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </header>

    <!-- ── THE PROBLEM ──────────────────────────────────────── -->
    <section class="section-light problem-section">
      <div class="wrap content-grid">
        <div class="cg-main">
          <h2>The Synonym Nightmare</h2>
          <p class="lead-text">
            Traditional auto-coders rely on rigid synonym lists or basic NLP. When a patient reports "my head feels like it's going to explode after taking the pill," legacy systems fail, routing the case to an expensive human medical coder for manual review.
          </p>
          <p>
            The Medivora MedDRA Coding Agent leverages semantic reasoning. It understands clinical context—differentiating between a 'fall' due to syncope versus a 'fall' due to environmental factors—and maps the verbatim text to the most precise Lowest Level Term (LLT) in the MedDRA hierarchy.
          </p>
        </div>
        <aside class="cg-side info-panel">
          <h4>Agent Profile</h4>
          <dl class="agent-specs">
            <dt>Data Inputs</dt>
            <dd>Verbatim reporter terms, full case narrative, patient medical history.</dd>
            <dt>Core AI Models</dt>
            <dd>Medical-grade LLMs (e.g., Med-PaLM 2) combined with Vector DB lookups.</dd>
            <dt>Key Outputs</dt>
            <dd>MedDRA LLT, PT, HLT, HLGT, and SOC codes with confidence scores.</dd>
            <dt>Standards</dt>
            <dd>MedDRA Terminology Guidelines (MSSO).</dd>
          </dl>
        </aside>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ── ARCHITECTURE ─────────────────────────────────────── -->
    <section class="section-dark arch-section">
      <div class="wrap">
        <h2>Agent Architecture &amp; Workflow</h2>
        <p class="section-desc">
          Instead of just mapping strings, the agent uses a Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) approach. It searches the official MedDRA dictionary and internal synonym lists, then reasons about the best fit based on the entire case context.
        </p>
        
        <div class="diagram-container">
          <pre class="mermaid">
flowchart LR
    classDef external fill:#1a1a1a,stroke:#333,stroke-width:1px,color:#888
    classDef input fill:#112a22,stroke:#10b981,stroke-width:1px,color:#fff
    classDef agent fill:#0a192f,stroke:#64ffda,stroke-width:1px,color:#fff
    classDef db fill:#2d1b2e,stroke:#e05c8a,stroke-width:1px,color:#fff
    classDef hitl fill:#3a2a00,stroke:#e8a838,stroke-width:1px,color:#fff
    classDef output fill:#112a22,stroke:#10b981,stroke-width:2px,color:#fff

    subgraph "Input"
        Verbatim["Verbatim AE Term<br>& Case Narrative"]:::external
    end

    subgraph "Reasoning Layer"
        direction TB
        Orchestrator(["Coding Orchestrator<br>(LLM)"]):::agent
        VectorDB[("MedDRA Dictionary<br>Vector Index")]:::db
        SynonymDB[("Client Specific<br>Synonym List")]:::db
    end

    subgraph "Action Layer"
        direction TB
        HighConf["High Confidence (>90%)<br>Auto-Code"]:::output
        LowConf["Low Confidence (<90%)<br>Human Review Queue"]:::hitl
        SafetyDB["Argus / Vault Safety"]:::output
    end

    Verbatim --> Orchestrator
    Orchestrator -- "Semantic Search" --> VectorDB
    Orchestrator -- "Exact Match" --> SynonymDB
    
    VectorDB --> Orchestrator
    SynonymDB --> Orchestrator

    Orchestrator -- "Determines Confidence" --> HighConf
    Orchestrator -- "Determines Confidence" --> LowConf

    HighConf --> SafetyDB
    LowConf -- "SME Selects Code" --> SafetyDB

    linkStyle default stroke:#444,stroke-width:2px;
          </pre>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ── CORE CAPABILITIES ────────────────────────────────── -->
    <section class="section-light caps-section">
      <div class="wrap">
        <h2 class="section-title-dark">Core Capabilities</h2>
        <div class="caps-grid">
          <div class="cap-card">
            <div class="cap-icon">1</div>
            <h3>Context-Aware Semantic Mapping</h3>
            <p>Does not just look at the verbatim string. It reads the patient's medical history and the full narrative to distinguish ambiguous terms (e.g., differentiating between a 'stroke' of luck in a psychiatric narrative and a 'stroke' as a CVA).</p>
          </div>
          <div class="cap-card">
            <div class="cap-icon">2</div>
            <h3>Confidence Scoring & Routing</h3>
            <p>Outputs a probabilistic confidence score for each coding decision. Configurable thresholds allow you to auto-code matches >95% confidence directly, while routing 94% and below to human coders.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="cap-card">
            <div class="cap-icon">3</div>
            <h3>Up-Versioning Automation</h3>
            <p>When the MSSO releases a new MedDRA version (e.g., v27.0 to v27.1), the agent can automatically scan the entire safety database and re-code obsolete LLTs to current terms instantly.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="cap-card">
            <div class="cap-icon">4</div>
            <h3>Multilingual Native Coding</h3>
            <p>Directly codes verbatim terms in French, German, Japanese, and 15+ other languages directly to the localized MedDRA dictionary without relying on lossy English pre-translations.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

  
    <!-- ── UNDER THE HOOD ───────────────────────────────────── -->
    <section class="section-dark under-hood-section">
      <div class="wrap">
        <span class="eyebrow">Under the Hood</span>
        <h2>Architectural Deep Dive</h2>
        
        <div class="uh-grid">
          <div class="uh-col">
            <h3>RAG & Semantic Search Architecture</h3>
            <p>Standard LLMs hallucinate MedDRA codes. The Coding Agent uses Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG): verbatim terms are embedded into vector space, compared against the embedded MedDRA dictionary, and top matches are retrieved for final LLM selection.</p>
            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
              <div class="code-header"><span class="code-dot r"></span><span class="code-dot y"></span><span class="code-dot g"></span><span class="code-title">search_meddra.py</span></div>
              <pre is:raw><code>def encode_and_search(verbatim_term, top_k=5):
    # 1. Generate embedding for verbatim term
    query_vector = embedding_model.encode(verbatim_term)
    
    # 2. Perform cosine similarity search in Pinecone/Milvus
    results = vector_db.query(
        vector=query_vector,
        namespace="meddra_v26",
        top_k=top_k,
        include_metadata=True
    )
    
    # 3. Return candidates to LLM for context
    return [match.metadata for match in results.matches]</code></pre>
            </div>
          </div>
          
          <div class="uh-col">
            <h3>Hierarchical Prompting</h3>
            <p>The agent is instructed to reason through the MedDRA hierarchy to ensure clinical logic, moving from System Organ Class (SOC) down to Lowest Level Term (LLT).</p>
            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
              <div class="code-header"><span class="code-dot r"></span><span class="code-dot y"></span><span class="code-dot g"></span><span class="code-title">system_prompt.txt</span></div>
              <pre is:raw><code>You are an expert Medical Coder.
Given a verbatim term and a list of dictionary candidates, select the most accurate MedDRA LLT.

REASONING TRACE REQUIRED:
1. Identify the likely System Organ Class (SOC).
2. Evaluate candidates based on High Level Group Term (HLGT) and High Level Term (HLT).
3. Select the Preferred Term (PT) that best captures the core medical concept.
4. Select the final Lowest Level Term (LLT).

Do NOT "up-code" (select a more severe term than reported) or "down-code" (select a less severe term).</code></pre>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="guardrails-box">
          <h3>Guardrails & Failure Modes</h3>
          <ul class="guardrail-list">
            <li><strong>Ambiguous Verbatims:</strong> Terms like "felt bad" lack specificity. The agent is trained to select a generic LLT (e.g., "Malaise") and flag the term for querying rather than guessing the underlying pathology.</li>
            <li><strong>Medical vs. Social History:</strong> The agent must distinguish between "patient had a stroke [medical history]" and "patient suffered a stroke [adverse event]" based on syntactic context.</li>
            <li><strong>Confidence Thresholds:</strong> If the cosine similarity of the best MedDRA match is below a defined threshold (e.g., 0.75), the agent refuses to code the term and queues it for a human medical coder.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

      </div>
    </section>

  
